#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3c48f82-c3ed-5014-b1f5-cd9e8de542f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Joint CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "a4b51a4b-0571-55e9-a3ea-881ffce4839b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10303" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$, find $F\\left(2,1\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i)\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(2, 1) &= P(X \\le 2, Y \\le 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to\n\n$$\nX \\leq 2, Y \\leq 1\n$$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(2, 1) &= ∑_(x \\le 2)∑_(y \\le 1)f(x, y) \\\\\n&= f(1,-2) + f(1,-1) + f(1, 0) \\\\\n&= 0.1 + 0.3 + 0.05 \\\\\n&= 0.45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161691 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162125 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02f8da68-ea1e-5f20-8f1a-7a38508e1f1f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "115c9ea4-0415-5249-8a4f-a7113e022681" :question/math-academy-id "e-10304" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y)$, where\n\n$F(5,4) = 0.9, \\quad F(5,2) = 0.2, \\quad F(3,4) = 0.5, \\quad F(3,2) = 0.1$,\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_X = \\{1, 3, 5,7\\}, \\quad S_Y = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\}$.\n\nFind $P(3 < X \\leq 5, 2 < Y \\leq 4)$." :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y)$, we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\neq 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y$, the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS = S_X \\times S_Y = \\{1, 3, 5,7 \\} \\times \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in the joint support.\n\n![](images/f0/f02b36afea872b4848a667039a5cbdb7f01c32853da61ed18df5c31329a3322a.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/41/412d3a5d68c8917d23e3ec3bb86646ee25cf1fac0f65227e7a2347831f484da7.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,4)$.\n2. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}(3,4)}$.\n3. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}(3,2)}$. This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3 < X \\le 5, 2 < Y \\le 4) &= P(X \\le 5, Y \\le 4)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](5,4)[/math]) \\\\\n&=-P(X \\le 5, Y \\le 2)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]) - P(X \\le 3, Y \\le 4)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](3,4)[/math]) \\\\\n&= + P(X \\le 3, Y \\le 2)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](3,2)[/math]) \\\\\n&= F(5, 4) - F(5, 2) - F(3, 4) + F(3, 2) \\\\\n&= 0.9 - 0.2 - 0.5 + 0.1 \\\\\n&= 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161696 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162130 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b8dd2d-ecaa-58bf-baa3-72dc8f37523f" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Joint CDF Geometrically"}] :question/id #uuid "28277187-2842-5b7a-bfee-672bd4f08aca" :question/math-academy-id "e-10336" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y)$, where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_X = \\{1, 2, 3, 4 \\}, \\quad S_Y = \\{2, 3, 4 \\}$.\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x > 4, y < 2$." :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\neq 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y$, the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS = S_X \\times S_Y = \\{1, 2, 3, 4 \\} \\times \\{2, 3, 4 \\}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x > 4, y < 2$, and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/81/81d50089329eaa369b3074b58918d68d6c55fac2ffb61f965e162ace24156ef7.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the points that lie inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nS \\cap \\Delta = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nSo there are no points in our region $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i) = \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in \\emptyset} f(x, y) = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161697 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97d9a7a-fa12-5d8d-8958-39625f117979" :knowledge-point/title "Further Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "0b991ee4-a4b3-55b4-93d1-77936f5b3067" :question/math-academy-id "e-10337" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y)$, where\n\n$F(8,3) = 0.9, \\qquad F(6,3) = 0.7, \\qquad F(4,3) = 0.4$,\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_X = \\{2, 4, 6, 8\\}, \\qquad S_Y = \\{1, 3, 5 \\}$.\n\nFind $P(X \\gt 6, Y \\leq 3)$." :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y)$, we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\neq 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y$, the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS = S_X \\times S_Y = \\{2, 4, 6, 8 \\} \\times \\{1, 3, 5 \\}\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in the joint support and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/91/91a0796929c7d023dd3a7110458ec2b9b3c365b8ba433c001707155c65cf4e28.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same points in the joint support as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(8,3)$ and top-left corner at $(6,3)$, where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/80/80d02d152f41e88c9533d06887a925a6acd9438924b36a3c1268e04d150ea1ac.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,3)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,3)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/d0/d094730505c58c936e5d4313ca9a9fc9c6ff9fd117f591a9382ef0719f8b8df3.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 6, Y \\le 3) &= P(X \\le 8, Y \\le 3)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](8,3)[/math]) - P(X \\le 6, Y \\le 3)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](6,3)[/math]) \\\\\n&= F(8, 3) - F(6, 3) \\\\\n&= 0.9 - 0.7 \\\\\n&= 0.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3c48f82-c3ed-5014-b1f5-cd9e8de542f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "863b10fe-f745-5566-ae64-5eb30b06c2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159669} {:answer/id #uuid "8a944662-c5dd-5606-9c9f-6709fa172b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159670} {:answer/id #uuid "a76f7163-9ca0-5f82-af73-cb015fc87e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159671} {:answer/id #uuid "c622b1a2-e96f-5222-bc89-4198a6a07bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159672} {:answer/id #uuid "21f58f58-8d2d-5c6a-901a-783e42784a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159673}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f03c2b00-d99f-52aa-a91b-33654f63b2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159668}] :question/id #uuid "579f1fda-9534-54f2-9a64-f554fdec8e40" :question/math-academy-id "q-145559" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x, y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$, find $F(2, 4)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162125 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02f8da68-ea1e-5f20-8f1a-7a38508e1f1f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8286e00e-0bb7-535d-81bc-15343aa5865e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159681} {:answer/id #uuid "c606afbf-d1f8-59a6-9616-2331533ee4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159682} {:answer/id #uuid "3fec6d08-8dab-55fc-a360-d34c5b7ad604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.79" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159683} {:answer/id #uuid "44983ca7-13d4-5d8e-93c5-7e72a615c302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159684} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa40e29-df44-55bd-a4df-2ae7c68e22bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159685}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d351d2dc-8505-5b9f-bcef-941a0c4aa318" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159680}] :question/id #uuid "c02655cf-ccfa-5e30-b9b0-9dbde84f8d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-145619" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n\n$F(10, 9) = 0.72, F(10, 6) = 0.3, F(8, 9) = 0.15, F(8, 6) = 0.08$,\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_{X} = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}, S_{Y} = {3, 6, 9, 12}$.\n\nFind $P(8 < X \\le 10, 6 < Y \\le 9)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
